  "text": "First where are we going wrong? We need housing in the cities, where the people and the jobs are. Instead we build outward, paving over farmland at the edge of town sealing soil that rain can't drain through, wiring in a car commute for everyone who moves there. Germany's own target is under THIRTY hectares of new land a day by 2030. We're sitting at FIFTY. So much for the target. Meanwhile, there's an obvious place to put those homes, and we're standing on it: the roofs we already have. The studies reckon about one point one MILLION apartments could go on top of existing housing blocks alone up to one and a half million if you add offices, supermarkets, parking decks. No new land, right where it's needed. That's Aufstockung building on top of what's already there. So why does almost none of it happen? The usual suspects height limits, parking rules, owners who can't agree. But mostly because actually pulling it off is harder than it looks.",
